// Shape reference for Form 7c — a new mode inside .claude/hooks/ArchHook.java.
//
// A mode is NOT a new file. Enforcement stays in one executable, which step 7 of
// `project-bootstrap` copies whole into the generated project; a second hook file would
// have to be copied, registered and kept in sync on its own, and the first one to fall
// out of sync fails without a word.
//
// Three pieces, in this order:
//   1. a `case` in the dispatch in `main`
//   2. the method, with the Javadoc that carries the "why this is a hook" record
//   3. the lists it reads, in .claude/schemas/extensions.json — never in the Java
//
// Then register it: ../templates/hook-entry.json.example.

// ── 1. dispatch, in main ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Next to the existing modes. `mode` defaults to "check" when no argument is given.

            switch (mode) {
                case "check"  -> check(filePath(stdin));
                // ...
                case "<mode>" -> <mode>();          // ← the new one
            }

// ── 2. the method ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /**
     * <One line: what this refuses to let through.>
     *
     * <p><Why this is a hook and not prose: the failure that was actually observed, and
     * the form that was rejected — usually a rule or a line in CLAUDE.md, which the
     * model may ignore. Name the event that invokes it. Three sentences; this Javadoc is
     * the level-1 record for Form 7c, since JSON has nowhere to put one.>
     *
     * <p>Design: .claude/decisions/NNNN-<slug>.md
     */
    static void <mode>() {
        Map<String, Object> sch = asMap(Json.parse(readOrNull(ROOT.resolve(SCHEMA_FILE))));
        if (sch == null) return;

        // Every name, path and pattern comes from extensions.json — invariant 10.
        // A list hardcoded here has two owners and diverges on the first edit.
        List<String> subjects = asStrList(get(sch, "<block>", "<list>"));

        List<String> errors = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String subject : subjects) {
            // ... the check
        }

        if (errors.isEmpty()) return;

        // stderr is the entire user interface of a blocking hook: it is all the person
        // who just got stopped will read. Name the rule, the file, and the way out.
        err("❌ <what was refused> — " + errors.size() + " problem(s)");
        for (String e : errors) err(e);
        err("");
        err("<what to do instead, concretely>");
        System.exit(2);          // 2 blocks. Any other non-zero is a non-blocking error.
    }

// ── 3. the data, in .claude/schemas/extensions.json ─────────────────────────────
//
//   "<block>": {
//     "$comment": "Read by ArchHook.java `<mode>`. Why this list is data and not Java:
//                  invariant 10 — the runtime ignores what it does not recognize, so a
//                  list with two owners diverges silently. Design: .claude/decisions/NNNN-<slug>.md",
//     "<list>": []
//   }
//
// ── before reporting it done ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   java .claude/hooks/ArchHook.java <mode>     ← run it once by hand.
//   java .claude/hooks/ArchHook.java schema     ← validates the registration.
//   java .claude/hooks/ArchHook.java doctor     ← confirms the count.
//
// A mode that throws exits 0 through the top-level catch in `main` and looks like it
// passed. Running it by hand is the only thing that catches that.
